# Titan Orbit — educational commenting standard (all files)

The project owner edits code by hand and learns by reading source. **Comments are part of the product**, not an afterthought. Every file you touch should read like a **well-written tutorial**: clear structure, patient explanations, and inline teaching at the points where confusion usually happens.

This rule is the **single source of truth** for comment style and density **across the entire project** — ECS sim, NetCode, UI, GameObject bridges, data assets, editor tools, services, input, camera, diagnostics, and tests. Architecture behavior lives in `titan-orbit-ship-simulation.mdc`; **how to document code lives here only**.

**Default expectation:** If you add or change code, the resulting file should be **beautifully commented** — someone with basic C# but little Unity/multiplayer experience can follow it without external docs.

---

## North star

```
Open any .cs file
  → File summary explains what this file is for and where it sits in the game
  → Every type, field group, and non-trivial member is introduced
  → Every method explains purpose, when it runs, inputs, outputs, side effects
  → Inside methods: section headers + inline comments on steps, branches, loops, and API calls
  → Domain terms defined on first use; standard vs Titan-Orbit choices labeled
```

**When editing:** Do not comment only the diff. Bring the **whole method, type, or logical block** up to this standard. If you touch a 50-line method with no comments, comment the full method before you finish.

---

## Audience and tone

Write for someone who:
- Knows basic C# (classes, methods, `if`, loops) but may not know Unity lifecycle, DOTS, NetCode, or multiplayer jargon.
- Cannot infer *why* a line exists from naming alone.
- Wants to learn **what things are called**, **what they do**, and **whether this is normal industry practice or a Titan Orbit custom choice**.

Tone: **patient teacher**, not expert shorthand. Plain language first. Define acronyms on first use in each file. Short sentences. Prefer "we" or "this method" over passive voice.

**Beautiful comments** means:
- Scannable layout (`// --- Section ---`, blank line between phases).
- Each comment adds **what**, **why**, or **when** — not a restatement of the identifier.
- Paired systems named (`Paired with ShipMovementSystem on the server`).
- Surprises called out (`// Intentional: we skip X because Y`).

---

## Comment layers (use every layer that applies)

| Layer | Where | Required content |
|-------|--------|------------------|
| **File** | Top `/// <summary>` on primary type | 3–6 sentences: role in the game, client/server/editor, reads/writes, neighbors in the pipeline, link to paired files if any. |
| **Type** | `/// <summary>` on every class, struct, enum, interface | What it represents in gameplay or tech terms; who creates/owns it. |
| **Members** | Fields, properties, constants, events, serialized `[SerializeField]` | What it holds, units if any, who sets it, lifecycle (scene load, spawn, per-frame). |
| **Method** | `/// <summary>` on every method (public, private, static) | Purpose, when called (Awake, fixed step, RPC, button click), inputs/outputs, side effects, failure modes. Use `<param>` / `<returns>` when not obvious. |
| **Section** | `// --- Name ---` inside methods and long types | One header per logical phase (cache refs → query ECS → apply to proxy). |
| **Inline** | Above or beside lines and small blocks | What this step does, why this branch, what this variable means **right now**. |
| **Closing** | End of complex methods (optional) | One line if helpful: what state the world is in after this method returns. |

**Density inside methods:** Comment the **flow**. For a typical method:
- **1–5 lines:** at least one inline comment if anything is non-obvious; otherwise a one-line method summary may suffice.
- **6–15 lines:** section header(s) + inline comments on each meaningful step.
- **16+ lines:** section headers for every phase + inline on branches, loops, math, and external calls.

Do **not** leave multi-line blocks with zero explanation. Do **not** add noise on truly empty lines or closing braces alone.

---

## Label standard vs custom

Tag comments where the reader might think "is this just Unity?" or "is this our game logic?":

| Tag | Meaning |
|-----|---------|
| `[STANDARD]` | Normal C#, software engineering, or widely used patterns |
| `[UNITY]` | Unity engine lifecycle, serialization, scenes, prefabs, UGUI |
| `[ECS/DOTS]` | Entities, components, systems, jobs, baking |
| `[NETCODE]` | NetCode for Entities: ghosts, prediction, RPCs, replication |
| `[PHYSICS]` | Unity Physics package behavior |
| `[TITAN-ORBIT]` | Custom design for this game — explain the game reason |
| `[HYBRID]` | Bridge between ECS sim and GameObject presentation (this project pattern) |
| `[EDITOR]` | Editor-only tooling; not in player/server builds |
| `[LEGACY]` | Old path kept temporarily — say what replaces it |

Example:
```csharp
// [NETCODE] GhostOwnerIsLocal — true only for the ship this player controls on this machine.
// [TITAN-ORBIT] We read presentation pose here, not raw sim — see ship-simulation rule §4.
```

